      "seconds": 75.726,
      "answer": "**Three-Part Study Guide: Pump Operation Procedure**\n\n### Part 1: Inspection\n\nBefore starting the pump, inspect the inlet for debris and check that the pressure gauge reads zero.\n\n**Practice Question:** What is the purpose of inspecting the inlet for debris?\n\n### Part 2: Operation\n\n1. Open the inlet valve before starting the pump.\n2. Start the pump and monitor the pressure gauge.\n3. Stop the pump if pressure exceeds 90 kPa.\n\n**Practice Question:** What is the maximum allowed pressure during pump operation?\n\n### Part 3: Shutdown\n\n1. Turn the motor off.\n2. Close the inlet valve.\n3. Record the final pressure.\n4. Do not open the casing while the pump is pressurized.\n\n**Short Answer Key:**\n\n1. What is the purpose of inspecting the inlet for debris?\nAnswer: To ensure the inlet is clear of debris and the pressure gauge reads zero.\n2. What is the maximum allowed pressure during pump operation?\nAnswer: 90 kPa.\n3. Why should the casing not be opened during shutdown?\nAnswer: To avoid pressurization and potential safety risks.\n\n> Coverage check: 3 of 3 source sections have no linked substantive passage in this answer. The response may be incomplete; full coverage is not verified.",
